# Pinned from the real red run of 2026-08-05 with the injection applied.
#
# The injection removes ONE thing: the `close_done_request` push that writes
# the flag's value to the close tx's `VERDICT` key. Everything else survives —
# the flag parses, `--help` documents it, the refusal prints the corrected
# remedy naming it. So the operator follows self-contained instructions,
# `dispatch-close --verdict approve` reports success, and the merge is refused
# anyway. That is the shape of a fix that changed only the prose, and it is
# why the acceptance test walks the loop instead of asserting the flag is
# accepted.
exit: 101

# THE DEFECT: a documented remedy that records nothing the gate can read. This
# is the FIRST failing assertion under this injection — the earlier refusal-
# text assertions all pass, which is exactly the point.
contains: --verdict must record the same VERDICT property the gate reads

# The remedy WAS followed and DID report success: the panic dumps the tx log,
# and the reviewer's terminal tx is in it — carrying no VERDICT. Without this
# line a replay that simply failed to dispatch a reviewer at all would read as
# this defect reproducing.
contains: :TYPE:         reviewer.done

# One test, and it is this one. A binary that collapses wholesale is a
# different failure and has not reproduced the defect this artifact claims.
contains: test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed
